From eeb81e697e07c2f21044f258b8f01102ce474064 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:32:29 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: hoist per-x worldX compression math out of the y loop

Real 486DX2-50 data (RENDERDB.LOG, refetched from the same run that
confirmed patch 0014's present-path fix) showed the render loop holding
steady at ~63-102ms/frame across two rounds while blit and present both
dropped sharply -- now the single largest named per-frame cost
remaining (blowup+blit+present down to ~56ms combined this round vs.
render loop's ~73ms).

Root cause, found by reading the render loop directly: `worldX` (game.
cpp, the perspective-compression math converting a screen column `x`
into a world-space sample column) depends only on `x`, `drawDX`
(per-frame, not per-pixel), and `playerX` -- NOT on `y`. But the
original `for(y) for(x))` loop recomputed it, including two genuinely
expensive transcendental calls (`tan()` and `pow()`) for every pixel
outside a small 10px no-compression zone near the player, on every
single `(x,y)` pair -- redundantly repeating the same per-x computation
`height` (12) times per frame. That's up to 1200 tan/pow call pairs per
frame when only 100 are ever actually distinct.

Fix: hoist the whole worldX computation (and the `intWorldX`/`aWeight`/
`bWeight` linear-interpolation values derived from it, and the
`maxWorldX`/`minWorldX` tracking, which only ever read x-dependent
values that don't vary across y) into a once-per-x precompute pass
before the y loop, caching the results in three small heap arrays
(grow-only static buffers, same pattern as blowUp.cpp's screenRowBuf
fix) that the y loop then just looks up. Same math, same results --
this is a pure hoist, not a change to the compression formula itself
(deliberately scoped narrowly: change what's recomputed, not how it's
computed, to keep this patch easy to verify against the original).
Cuts the tan/pow call count 12x on this port's current 100x12 image
(1200/frame -> 100/frame).

Verified under DOSBox-X: builds; visually confirmed clean, correct
scrolling and edge-compression behavior across several seconds of
gameplay (two screenshots a few seconds apart show the character and
world advancing normally, same compressed-edge visual pattern as every
prior smoke test in this series) -- this patch changes actual per-pixel
world-sample coordinates, so this comparison was deliberately more
careful than a plain "does it still boot" check. RENDERDB.LOG dropped
on this host too (was ~63-113ms in earlier DOSBox-X captures, now
~14-20ms), though DOSBox-X timing was never authoritative for magnitude
in this investigation -- confirms the mechanism, not the real-hardware
payoff. Clean boot/play/ESCAPE-quit, no hang. Real-hardware
confirmation on the 486DX2-50 (fps number plus a repeat RENDERDB.LOG
capture) is the next step.
